    <name>
      <text>Differentiating the function f(x) using the chain rule.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Differentiate the following. </p>
<p>\(\quad f(x) = {@fx@}\)</p>
<p>\(\quad f'(x) =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To differentiate the function \(f(x) ={@fx@}\) with respect to \(x\), we will use the chain rule. </p>
<p>The chain rule states that if you have a function \(g(u)\) inside another function \(f(v)\), then the derivative of \(f(g(u))\) with respect to \(u\) is given by:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle 
\frac{d}{du}(f(g(u))) = f'(g(u)) \cdot g'(u)
\)</p>
<p>In this case, let \(u = {@u@}\), and \(v = \sin(u)\). Then we have:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle 
f(x) = v = \sin(u)
\)</p>
<p>Now, let's calculate the derivatives:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Derivative of \(v = \sin(u)\) with respect to \(u\):</li>
  <p>\( \displaystyle 
     \frac{dv}{du} = \cos(u)
     \)</p>
  <li>Derivative of \(u = {@u@}\) with respect to \(x\):</li>
  <p>\( \displaystyle 
     \frac{du}{dx} = {@diff_u@}
     \)</p>
</ol>
<p>Now, using the chain rule, we can find the derivative of \(f(x)\):</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle 
\frac{df}{dx} = \frac{dv}{du} \cdot \frac{du}{dx} = \cos\left({@u@}\right) \cdot \left({@diff_u@}\right)
\)</p>
<p>So, the derivative of \(f(x) = {@fx@}\) with respect to \(x\) is:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle 
f'(x) = {@ta@}
\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
